"cprStep1": "Check for a response — tap the shoulders and call out loudly.",
"cprStep2": "Ask someone to get emergency help and an AED.",
"cprStep3": "Place the heel of one hand in the centre of the chest, with the other hand on top.",
"cprStep4": "Push hard and fast, 5–6 cm deep, at 100–120 compressions per minute.",
"cprStep5": "Do not stop until the person moves or professional help arrives.",
"cprWarning": "Perform CPR only if the person is unresponsive and not breathing normally.",

"bleedingStep1": "Apply firm direct pressure to the wound immediately using a clean cloth, sterile dressing, or your hand if nothing else is available.",
"bleedingStep2": "Keep continuous pressure on the wound. Do not repeatedly remove the dressing to check the bleeding.",
"bleedingStep3": "If blood soaks through, place another dressing on top and continue applying pressure.",
"bleedingStep4": "Raise the injured arm or leg above heart level only if no fracture is suspected.",
"bleedingStep5": "Get emergency medical help if bleeding cannot be controlled or is life-threatening.",
"bleedingWarning": "Do not remove objects deeply embedded in a wound. Apply pressure around the object and wait for professional help.",

"chokingStep1": "Ask the person if they are choking. If they can cough or speak, encourage them to keep coughing.",
"chokingStep2": "If they cannot cough, speak, or breathe, give up to 5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades.",
"chokingStep3": "If the object does not come out, give up to 5 abdominal thrusts for adults and children over 1 year old.",
"chokingStep4": "Continue alternating 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts until the blockage is removed or the person becomes unresponsive.",
"chokingStep5": "If the person becomes unresponsive, get emergency help and begin CPR if they are not breathing normally.",
"chokingWarning": "Do not use abdominal thrusts on infants. For a pregnant person, use chest thrusts instead. Use age-appropriate first-aid techniques.",

"burnsStep1": "Move the person away from the heat source only if it is safe to do so.",
"burnsStep2": "Cool the burned area under clean, cool running water for 10–20 minutes.",
"burnsStep3": "Remove rings, watches, and loose clothing before swelling begins, but do not remove anything stuck to the burn.",
"burnsStep4": "Loosely cover the burn with a sterile non-stick dressing or clean plastic wrap.",
"burnsStep5": "Get urgent medical care for deep, chemical, electrical, large, or facial burns.",
"burnsWarning": "Do not apply toothpaste, butter, oils, creams, or ice directly onto a burn.",

"fractureStep1": "Tell the person to remain still and avoid moving the injured body part.",
"fractureStep2": "Support the injured limb with towels, clothing, or a splint only if you are trained and it is safe.",
"fractureStep3": "Apply a wrapped cold pack to reduce swelling. Never place ice directly on the skin.",
"fractureStep4": "If a spine injury is suspected, keep the head, neck, and back aligned. Do not move the person unless there is immediate danger.",
"fractureStep5": "Get emergency medical help for severe pain, deformity, heavy bleeding, or a suspected spine injury.",
"fractureWarning": "Do not attempt to straighten broken bones or move a person with a suspected spinal injury.",

"shockStep1": "Lay the person flat on their back unless an injury or breathing difficulty prevents it.",
"shockStep2": "If no injury is suspected, raise the feet about 15–30 cm unless a different position is more comfortable for breathing.",
